 questions: [
  ["A 55-year-old develops sudden weakness of the right side of the face. He cannot wrinkle his right forehead or close his right eye. Limb strength and speech are normal. The most likely diagnosis is:","Right-sided Bell's palsy",["Left hemisphere stroke","Right hemisphere stroke","Trigeminal neuralgia"],"Involvement of the forehead marks a lower motor neuron lesion of the facial nerve, as in Bell's palsy. A cortical stroke spares the forehead because the upper facial nucleus receives bilateral input, and trigeminal neuralgia is sensory pain, not weakness."],
  ["Parasympathetic fibres to the submandibular gland travel in the:","Chorda tympani",["Greater petrosal nerve","Lesser petrosal nerve","Auriculotemporal nerve"],"The chorda tympani carries preganglionic fibres from VII to the submandibular ganglion. The greater petrosal goes to the pterygopalatine ganglion (lacrimal and palatal glands); the lesser petrosal serves the parotid."],
  ["On asking a patient to protrude the tongue it deviates to the left. The nerve most likely damaged is the:","Left hypoglossal nerve",["Right hypoglossal nerve","Left lingual nerve","Left vagus nerve"],"The genioglossus on each side pushes the tongue forward and medially; with the left muscle paralysed the right one pushes the tongue toward the left, the side of the lesion. The lingual nerve is sensory only."],
  ["The afferent limb of the gag reflex is carried by the:","Glossopharyngeal nerve",["Vagus nerve","Chorda tympani of the facial nerve","Lingual nerve"],"IX supplies sensation to the posterior tongue and oropharynx; the efferent response through the pharyngeal muscles is via X."],
  ["A patient says ah and the uvula moves to the right. Which nerve is most likely affected?","Left vagus nerve",["Right vagus nerve","Left glossopharyngeal nerve","Left hypoglossal nerve"],"The levator veli palatini (vagus) on the intact side pulls the soft palate and uvula toward itself, away from the weak left side."],
  ["Taste from the posterior third of the tongue is carried by the:","Glossopharyngeal nerve",["Chorda tympani of the facial nerve","Lingual nerve","Hypoglossal nerve"],"IX carries both taste and general sensation from the posterior third; VII (chorda tympani) carries taste from the anterior two-thirds, and the lingual nerve carries general sensation there."],
  ["During an inferior alveolar block the needle advances its full length without contacting bone and the solution is deposited. The complication most likely to follow within minutes is:","Transient facial palsy on the injected side",["Trismus from medial pterygoid spasm","Permanent numbness of the lower lip","Haematoma of the pterygoid venous plexus"],"Without bone contact the needle tip can lie in the parotid, where the facial nerve branches are bathed in anaesthetic; the palsy lasts the duration of the drug. Trismus develops over days from medial pterygoid injury."],
  ["Which muscle of the soft palate is NOT supplied by the vagus nerve?","Tensor veli palatini",["Levator veli palatini","Palatoglossus","Musculus uvulae"],"The tensor veli palatini is supplied by the mandibular nerve via the nerve to medial pterygoid; the other palatal muscles are supplied through the pharyngeal plexus of the vagus."],
  ["A 24-year-old faints in the dental chair before an injection. Her pulse is 48 per minute and she is pale and sweating. The mechanism is best explained by:","Vagal discharge causing bradycardia and vasodilation",["Sympathetic discharge causing tachycardia and pallor","Hypoglycaemia reducing cerebral glucose","An allergic reaction releasing histamine"],"Vasovagal syncope is parasympathetic overactivity: the heart slows and vessels dilate, so cerebral perfusion falls. Sympathetic responses give tachycardia; anaphylaxis would add urticaria or wheeze."],
  ["Which of the following best explains hyperacusis in a patient with Bell's palsy?","Paralysis of stapedius removes damping of the ossicles",["Paralysis of tensor tympani tightens the eardrum","Loss of sensation in the external ear canal","Spasm of the muscles of facial expression"],"Stapedius, supplied by VII in the facial canal, dampens stapes movement; when paralysed, ordinary sounds seem loud. Tensor tympani is supplied by V3 and is unaffected."],
  ["The facial nerve passes through which gland without supplying it?","Parotid gland",["Submandibular gland","Sublingual gland","Lacrimal gland"],"The parotid is pierced by VII, which divides into its terminal branches within it, while its secretomotor supply comes from IX. This is why parotid tumours and parotid surgery threaten facial movement."],
  ["A surgeon plans an extraoral approach to the angle of the mandible. The incision is placed well below the lower border mainly to protect the:","Marginal mandibular branch of the facial nerve",["Lingual nerve","Inferior alveolar nerve at the mandibular foramen","Hypoglossal nerve in the submandibular triangle"],"The marginal mandibular branch loops below the mandibular border before reaching the depressor muscles of the lip; damage leaves an asymmetric smile. The lingual and inferior alveolar nerves are intraoral or intrabony."],
  ["Which muscle of the tongue is supplied by the vagus nerve rather than the hypoglossal nerve?","Palatoglossus",["Genioglossus","Hyoglossus","Styloglossus"],"Palatoglossus is developmentally a palatal muscle and is supplied by the vagus through the pharyngeal plexus; genioglossus, hyoglossus and styloglossus, like every intrinsic tongue muscle, are supplied by XII."],
  ["A 70-year-old develops brief, severe stabbing pain in the left throat and ear whenever he swallows. Oral examination is normal. The nerve most likely involved is the:","Glossopharyngeal nerve",["Mandibular division of the trigeminal","Facial nerve","Hypoglossal nerve"],"Glossopharyngeal neuralgia is triggered by swallowing and felt in the tonsillar fossa and ear, the sensory territory of IX. Trigeminal neuralgia affects the face and is triggered by touching the face or teeth."]
 ]
