 apply: {
  vignette: "A 29-year-old presents with a three-day swelling of the right cheek beside the nose, with obliteration of the nasolabial fold. Tooth 1.3 is grossly carious, non-vital and tender to percussion. Since this morning she has had a fever of 39 °C, a severe headache, and the right upper eyelid is swollen with the eye beginning to protrude. The most likely explanation for the eye signs is:",
  options: ["Retrograde spread through the valveless facial and ophthalmic veins to the cavernous sinus","Direct spread of pus through the orbital floor from the maxillary sinus","Allergic angio-oedema from the antibiotic she started","Compression of the facial nerve by the swelling"],
  answer: "Retrograde spread through the valveless facial and ophthalmic veins to the cavernous sinus",
  steps: [
   "The ask is the mechanism of the eye signs. Facts: a canine space infection from 1.3 (swelling beside the nose, lost nasolabial fold), then fever, headache, lid swelling and proptosis.",
   "The canine space sits in the danger triangle, drained by the angular and facial veins, which have no valves and connect to the superior ophthalmic vein and the cavernous sinus.",
   "Septic thrombophlebitis travelling backward along that route gives cavernous sinus thrombosis: proptosis, chemosis, ophthalmoplegia, V1 numbness, fever and headache. The picture matches.",
   "Cross out spread through the orbital floor: the sinus is not involved by a canine (the canine root sits anterior to the sinus) and bone erosion into the orbit would not produce headache and fever this fast.",
   "Cross out angio-oedema: it is painless, usually bilateral and not accompanied by fever and headache. Cross out facial nerve compression: VII palsy causes weakness, not protrusion of the eye.",
   "The survivor explains every finding and tells you what to do: this is a medical emergency needing hospital admission, intravenous antibiotics and drainage of the source."
  ]
 },
 teach: {
  prompt: "Using only the positions of the mylohyoid and buccinator attachments, predict where an abscess from 4.8, 4.5 and 1.6 (buccal roots) will present, and explain why the exam treats 4.8 as the most dangerous of the three.",
  model: "The apices of 4.8 lie below the mylohyoid line, so pus that breaks through the thin lingual plate enters the submandibular space; from there it can reach the lateral pharyngeal space and, bilaterally, become Ludwig's angina with airway loss, which is why 4.8 is the dangerous one. The apex of 4.5 lies above the mylohyoid line, so lingual perforation gives a sublingual swelling, and buccal perforation inside the buccinator gives a vestibular abscess. The buccal roots of 1.6 usually perforate the buccal plate inside the buccinator attachment and present as a vestibular abscess; if the apices lie above the attachment the pus enters the buccal space and the cheek swells."
 },
